
Every Skupper CRD has a Spec and a Status section.

The spec says what you want, while the Status shows 
what you currently have.

Users write to the Spec.
Controllers update the Status.

You can check on the status of a resource with 
commands like:

      kubectl get site      -o yaml
      kubectl get listener  -o yaml
      kubectl get connector -o yaml

Or, to just see part of the status:

      kubectl get site west -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'


List all Skupper-related CRDs:

      kubectl get crd | grep skupper

Describe a CRD:

      kubectl describe crd sites.skupper.io



Show the schema for the Spec section of the Listener CRD:
{
    kubectl get crd listeners.skupper.io -o yaml | yq '.spec.versions[0].schema.openAPIV3Schema.properties.spec'

    See what fields a Listener supports without reading the full CRD or source code.

    How it works:
    {
      1. kubectl get crd listeners.skupper.io
         get CRD for listeners

      2. -o yaml
         put it out as yaml

      3. | yq '...' 
         pipe to yq, a YAML processor

      4. .spec.versions[0]
         select the first version of the CRD

      5. .schema.openAPIV3Schema
         Goes into the OpenAPI validation schema

      6. .properties.spec
         Extracts only the schema definition for the spec field

    }
}





